{{also|Tirade}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtaɪreɪd/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|eɪd|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A long, [[angry]] or [[violent]] [[speech]]; a [[diatribe]].
#*{{quote-book|year=1898|author={{w|Winston Churchill (novelist)|Winston Churchill}}
|title={{w|The Celebrity}}|chapter=4
|passage=Mr. Cooke at once began a '''tirade''' against the residents of Asquith for permitting a sandy and generally disgraceful condition of the roads. So roundly did he vituperate the inn management in particular, and with such a loud flow of words, that I trembled lest he should be heard on the veranda.}}
#*{{quote-book|year=1918|author={{w|W. B. Maxwell}}|chapter=13
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1097634W The Mirror and the Lamp]
|passage=&ldquo;[…]&nbsp;They talk of you as if you were Croesus—and I expect the beggars sponge on you unconscionably.&rdquo; And Vickers launched forth into a '''tirade''' very different from his platform utterances. He spoke with extreme contempt of the dense stupidity exhibited on all occasions by the working classes.}}
# A section of [[verse]] concerning a single [[theme]]; a [[laisse]].

